    * cite29† Cube math primitives  L47:     * cite30† gridMET variance cube  L48:     * cite31† PRISM variance cube  L49:     * cite32† Fire event vase + climate merge (fire_plot)  L50:     * cite33† Sentinel-2 NDVI z-score cube  L51:     * cite34† Sentinel-2 correlation center  L52:     * cite35† Sentinel-2 tail dependence (center)  L53:     * cite36† Sentinel-2 tail dependence (manual)  L54:   *   Visualization  Visualization\nL55:     * cite37† Overview  L56:     * cite38† Cube viewer (v.plot)  L57:     * cite39† Maps (v.map)  L58:     * cite40† Suitability tubes  L59:   *   API  API\nL60:     * cite41† Overview  L61:     * cite42† Pipe  L62:     * cite43† Verbs  L63:     * cite44† Data  L64:     * cite45† Visualization  L65:     * cite46† Inventory (User)  L66:     * cite47† Inventory (Full / Dev)  L67:     * cite48† Reference  L68:   *   Contributing / Development  Contributing / Development\nL69:     * cite49† Contributing  L70:     * cite50† CI and Testing  L71:     * cite51† Cube viewer invariants  L72:     * cite52† Fire plot architecture  L73:     * cite53† Figure backend (v.plot)  L74:     * cite54† Viewer backend  L75:     * cite55† Legacy reference  L76:     * cite56† Public API & scope  L77:     * cite57† Project Scope  L78:     * cite58† Deprecation inventory  L79:   *   Design  Design\nL80:     * cite59† Spatial & CRS Dataset Contract  L81: Table of contents\nL82: \nL83:   * cite10† Installation  L84:   * cite11† Your First Cube  L85:   * cite12† Your First Pipeline  L86:   * cite13† Loading Real Datasets  L87:   * cite14† Scaling Up Without Changing Code  L88:   * cite15† Working With Large Datasets  L89:   * cite16† Common Pitfalls  L90:   * cite17† Where to go next  L91: # Getting Started\nL92: \nL93: This guide gets you to a first working cube and pipeline as quickly as possible, while showing how the same code scales up when datasets are large.\nL94: \nL95: ## Installation\nL96: \nL97: Install from PyPI:\nL98: \nL99:     pip install cubedynamics\nL100: \nL101: Or install the latest main branch:\nL102: \nL103:     pip install \"git+https://github.com/CU-ESIIL/cubedynamics.git@main\"\nL104: \nL105: CubeDynamics runs anywhere `xarray` runs: laptops, HPC clusters, or hosted notebooks.\nL106: ## Your First Cube\nL107: \nL108: Most users start by loading a cube from an existing dataset. For example, this creates a precipitation cube from PRISM:\nL109: \nL110:     import cubedynamics as cd\nL111: \nL112:     cube = cd.load_prism_cube(\nL113:         lat=40.0,\nL114:         lon=-105.25,\nL115:         start=\"2000-01-01\",\nL116:         end=\"2020-12-31\",\nL117:         variable=\"ppt\",\nL118:     )\nL119: \nL120: Inspect the structure to confirm dimensions and metadata:\nL121: \nL122:     cube.dims\nL123:     cube.shape\nL124:     cube.attrs\nL125: ## Your First Pipeline\nL126: \nL127: Pipelines are built with the pipe (`|`) operator and a grammar of verbs:\nL128: \nL129:     from cubedynamics import pipe, verbs as v\nL130: \nL131:     result = pipe(cube) | v.mean(dim=\"time\") | v.plot()\nL132: \nL133: This example computes the mean through time for every location in the cube and plots the result. You can swap in different verbs (e.g., `v.anomaly()`, `v.variance()`, `v.month_filter([6, 7, 8])`) without changing the pipeline structure.\nL134: ## Loading Real Datasets\nL135: \nL136: The same pattern works for other datasets shipped with CubeDynamics. The `load_prism_cube` call above can be expanded to longer time ranges or larger spatial domains, and other loaders follow the same conventions for inputs like latitude, longitude, date ranges, and variable names.\nL137: ## Scaling Up Without Changing Code\nL138: \nL139: Large requests automatically stream as VirtualCubes, so you do not have to rewrite your pipeline when datasets exceed memory. VirtualCubes:\nL140: \nL141:   * represent a cube without materializing it upfront\nL142:   * stream tiles of data through the same verbs\nL143:   * keep your code and semantics identical at small or large scales\nL144: ## Working With Large Datasets\nL145: \nL146: If you request a larger area of interest or longer date range, the loader silently returns a VirtualCube that streams tiles through the same verbs. You can inspect and control streaming when needed:\nL147:     ndvi = cd.ndvi(\nL148:         lat=40.0,\nL149:         lon=-105.25,\nL150:         start=\"1970\",\nL151:         end=\"2020\",\nL152:         streaming_strategy=\"virtual\",\nL153:         time_tile=\"5y\",\nL154:     )\nL155:     print(ndvi)           # shows that it is a VirtualCube\nL156:     ndvi.debug_tiles()    # prints time + space tiles\nL157:     ndvi.materialize()    # forces full load; only for small areas\nL158: \nL159: Try smaller `time_tile` values or reduced spatial bounds if you see slow progress or rate limits.\nL160: When a request is too large for a normal in-memory cube, CubeDynamics:\nL161: \nL162:   * splits the timeline into tiles (for example, five-year windows)\nL163:   * splits the area of interest into spatial tiles when needed\nL164:   * streams each tile through the verbs, tracking running statistics like variance or mean\nL165:   * returns a normal-looking DataArray/Dataset at the end\nL166: ## Common Pitfalls\nL167:   * Make sure the requested variable name matches the dataset.\nL168:   * Verify dimensions before running large analyses so operations occur over the intended axis.\nL169:   * Use streaming defaults for big pulls instead of forcing full materialization.\nL170:   * For event windows, request daily frequency (`freq=\"D\"`)—monthly codes like `\"MS\"`/`\"ME\"` over short ranges can return an empty time axis.\nL171:   * Leave `allow_synthetic=False` unless you explicitly want demo data; provenance (`source`, `is_synthetic`, `backend_error`) on cubes will confirm what you received.\nL172:   * A \"streaming backend unavailable\" warning means CubeDynamics fell back to a download backend. Install optional dependencies or check network access before re-running if you need streaming.\nL173: ## Where to go next\nL174: \nL175:   * Concepts\nL176:   * cite4†What is a cube? L177:   * cite60†Pipe & verbs L178:   * cite6†VirtualCubes L179: \nL180:   * Recipes / How-tos\nL181: \nL182:   * cite61†Minimal NDVI vignette L183:   * cite62†NDVI anomalies L184:   * cite63†Climate variance L185:   * cite64†Correlation cubes L186: \nL187:   * Visualization\nL188: \nL189:   * cite38†Cube viewer ( v.plot ) L190:   * cite39†Map viewer ( v.map ) L191: \nL192:   * API Reference\nL193: \nL194:   * cite41†API overview L195: CubeDynamics provides a unified, cube-native way to work with spatiotemporal environmental data—simple enough for quick exploration, powerful enough for large-scale scientific analysis.\nL196: \nL197: 2026-03-28\nL198: \nL199: Copyright \\u00a9 2024\nL200: \nL201: Made with cite65† Material for MkDocs †squidfunk.github.io "
